  "query_preview": "..Mostly clear skies this morning across our forecast \narea as high pressure aloft remains near the AZ/NM border. Moisture \nlevels are still a bit on the drier side east of Tucson with PWAT \nvalues from 0.8\" to 1 inch, with higher moisture from Tucson \nwestward with PWATs from 1 to 1.4 inches. This moisture will \ncontinue to get reinforced this morning from Sonora. For later \ntoday, the CAMs are a bit more aggressive on convective development \nthan 24 hours ago. The high will remain near the AZ/NM border so the \nplacement isn't great but enough moisture will continue to move in \nunder the high to result in isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms this afternoon and evening, primarily from Sells \neastward. In fact, the HREF is depicting a few spots south and east \nof Tucson with a greater than 10 percent neighborhood probability \n(40km) of 50 kt wind gusts. Given the continued extreme heat with \nDCAPE values in excess of 1500 J/KG today, wind will be the primary \nthreat from thunderstorms with locally heavy rain the secondary \nthreat. \n\nWe'll settle into the more active monsoon pattern Friday through \nMonday as the mid/upper level high shifts east into New Mexico and \nalso simultaneously becomes more elongated from east-west. This \npattern will allow for a more sustained southeasterly to southerly \nlower to mid level flow. Expect sustained elevated PWAT's ranging \nfrom 1.1\" to 1.8\", lower east and higher west or about 110 to 160 \npercent of normal. While PoPs will remain in the 30 to 70 percent \nrange during this period, there will be day-to-day variability in \nconvection and it's a bit hard to decipher which will be the most \nactive days. Saturday and Sunday are the most favored days right \nnow. Expect typical monsoon threats through the weekend including \ngusty winds and locally heavy rain with potential for areas of flash \nflooding. \n\nBy next Tuesday and Wednesday, there are some subtle signs of modest \ndrying as the flow becomes a bit more southwesterly but confidence \nremains low. Meanwhile, the eastern Pacific tropics are active so \nwe'll be keeping close tabs on that and any moisture impacts for us \nas we head into and through September. \n\nThe Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Saturday with the \nhottest temperatures through Friday. Temperatures will lower to near \nnormal levels Sunday into next week, with perhaps Monday even coming \nin slightly below normal. That will be a welcome change.",
